  "body": "Found by the review of PR 1469 (lathe chuck fixtures as collision geometry).\n\nWhat happens: the chuck's round outer faces are drawn with flats placed 0.12 % outside the true circle, so the collision solid never falls inside the real part. The bore wall is drawn the other way round: its flats leave the wall up to 0.12 % of the bore radius short of the true bore (0.012 mm on a 20 mm collet bore), so the modelled chuck has slightly less material there than the real one. The code comment at that place claims the opposite (that the bore is conservative).\n\nA second gap: the sim test that pins the envelope probes points 0.5 to 1 mm from the rim and the jaw tip, so a solid shrunk by less than 0.5 mm there would still pass.\n\nExpected: every face of a fixture's collision solid is on or outside the real surface (for a bore: on or inside the true bore radius), the comment says what the code does, and the test pins the envelope to the tessellation allowance, not to half a millimetre.\n\nDone means: the bore polygon is inscribed in the true bore (or the allowance is applied inward), the comment is corrected, and the envelope test probes within the stated allowance of the rim, jaw tip and bore wall and fails on a solid shrunk by more than it.\n\nApplies once PR 1469 is on main (the code is not on main before the next cut).\n",
